DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These macros define and operate on doubly linked lists.

       In the macro definitions, _T_Y_P_E is the name of a user‐defined structure,
       that must contain a field of type _L_I_S_T___E_N_T_R_Y, named _N_A_M_E.  The argument
       _H_E_A_D_N_A_M_E is the name of a user‐defined structure that must be  declared
       using the macro LLIISSTT__HHEEAADD().

   CCrreeaattiioonn
       A list is headed by a structure defined by the LLIISSTT__HHEEAADD() macro.  This
       structure  contains  a single pointer to the first element on the list.
       The elements are doubly linked so that an arbitrary element can be  re‐
       moved  without  traversing  the list.  New elements can be added to the
       list after an existing element, before an existing element, or  at  the
       head of the list.  A _L_I_S_T___H_E_A_D structure is declared as follows:

           LIST_HEAD(HEADNAME, TYPE) head;

       where  _s_t_r_u_c_t  _H_E_A_D_N_A_M_E is the structure to be defined, and _s_t_r_u_c_t _T_Y_P_E
       is the type of the elements to be linked into the list.  A  pointer  to
       the head of the list can later be declared as:

           struct HEADNAME *headp;

       (The names _h_e_a_d and _h_e_a_d_p are user selectable.)

       LLIISSTT__EENNTTRRYY()  declares  a  structure  that connects the elements in the
       list.

       LLIISSTT__HHEEAADD__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR() evaluates to an initializer for the list _h_e_a_d.

       LLIISSTT__IINNIITT() initializes the list referenced by _h_e_a_d.

       LLIISSTT__EEMMPPTTYY() evaluates to true if there are no elements in the list.

   IInnsseerrttiioonn
       LLIISSTT__IINNSSEERRTT__HHEEAADD() inserts the new element _e_l_m at the head of the list.

       LLIISSTT__IINNSSEERRTT__BBEEFFOORREE() inserts the new element  _e_l_m  before  the  element
       _l_i_s_t_e_l_m.

       LLIISSTT__IINNSSEERRTT__AAFFTTEERR()  inserts the new element _e_l_m after the element _l_i_s_‐
       _t_e_l_m.

   TTrraavveerrssaall
       LLIISSTT__FFIIRRSSTT() returns the first element in the list, or NULL if the list
       is empty.

       LLIISSTT__NNEEXXTT() returns the next element in the list, or NULL  if  this  is
       the last.

       LLIISSTT__FFOORREEAACCHH() traverses the list referenced by _h_e_a_d in the forward di‐
       rection, assigning each element in turn to _v_a_r.

   RReemmoovvaall
       LLIISSTT__RREEMMOOVVEE() removes the element _e_l_m from the list.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       LLIISSTT__EEMMPPTTYY() returns nonzero if the list is empty, and zero if the list
       contains at least one entry.

       LLIISSTT__FFIIRRSSTT(),  and  LLIISSTT__NNEEXXTT()  return  a pointer to the first or next
       _T_Y_P_E structure, respectively.

       LLIISSTT__HHEEAADD__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR() returns an initializer that can be assigned  to
       the list _h_e_a_d.

